{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For any leaven or any honey you shall not burn from it as a fire-offering to HaShem.</b> The Rav, of blessed memory, wrote in the Guide for the Perplexed that the custom of the worshippers of idols was that in every meal-offering they would offer leaven, and they would mix honey into all their sacrifices; therefore He forbade them for the One on high. And a similar thing did our Rabbis of blessed memory say concerning a pillar (מצבה) — that it was chosen in the days of the Patriarchs, and afterward HaShem hated it because they made it a statute for idolatrous worship, as it is said (Deuteronomy 16): ״אשר שנא ה' אלהיך״." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the sacrifice is to atone thereby for all our sins, and were it not for the inciter and the seducer, which is the evil inclination, a person would not sin and would not need a sacrifice at all. And the leaven and the honey are the evil inclination itself, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said concerning leaven and matzah on Passover — a person must turn his heart away from the evil inclination. And this is what they said: ״לא תאכל עליו חמץ״ — with regard to the Passover sacrifice; and the Passover sacrifice was an atonement for the idolatry they served in Egypt; therefore He distances them from the evil inclination, so that they not return to idolatry. And honey too is the evil inclination, for דבש in gematria is אשה, and the woman is the evil inclination, for thus we find with the first man: when he sinned he said (Genesis 3:12) ״האשה אשר נתתה עמדי״ — meaning, the evil inclination that You created within me. And the woman said, ״הנחש השיאני,״ and this is the mystery of the serpent and the woman and the enmity. And therefore the leaven and the honey were distanced from the sacrifice, because two opposites cannot subsist in one subject; and furthermore, because he would be found immersing [in a mikveh] while a sheretz is in his hand — and enough for the one who understands." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the leaven and the honey allude to the attribute of judgment (מדת הדין), as is known from the language of חמץ. And because they are things that departed from their proper mixture, therefore they are distanced from the altar. On this account it says: ״לא תקטירו ממנו אשה לה',״ for since the intention is toward the unique Name, you shall not bake leaven and you shall not burn leaven and honey."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:23:09.824464353Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}